        {
          "question": "e-483",
          "field": null,
          "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
          "value": "Let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine that involves the $\\sin^2$ term: \n\n$$\n\\cos {2\\theta}=1-2\\sin^2\\theta.\n$$\n\nWe can rearrange the above formula as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2}\\left(1-\\cos {2\\theta} \\right )=\\sin^2\\theta.\n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta = 3x$, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2}\\left(1-\\cos {6x} \\right )=\\sin^2(3x).\n$$\n\nWe can now integrate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {sin}^{2}\u2061(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{2}(1-\\operatorname{cos}\u20616x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int (1-\\operatorname{cos}\u20616x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(x-\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{sin}\u20616x)+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}x-\\frac{1}{12}\\operatorname{sin}\u20616x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
          "category": "ma_capture",
          "file": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14008673/example-483.json",
          "file_sha256": "d4589f3407741b43cf9c39629536db1e81b0616e2a68d55c923258253899d4ab"
